1
00:00:00,118 --> 00:00:00,742
A quick word.

2
00:00:01,257 --> 00:00:04,179
The person you see in this video is an AI
clone of me.

3
00:00:04,712 --> 00:00:07,218
The ideas and the words, though, are all
mine.

4
00:00:07,679 --> 00:00:08,647
I’m Daniel Chan.

5
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:13,341
I teach at the Centre for Language Studies
at NUS, and I’ve been teaching French for

6
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:14,653
over twenty-five years.

7
00:00:15,295 --> 00:00:17,475
Today I want to talk about FluOLinGo.

8
00:00:18,135 --> 00:00:23,661
That’s capital F, O, L and G, and it stands
for Fluency On Linguistic Goals.

9
00:00:23,978 --> 00:00:28,790
It’s a platform for beginners learning
French, and I built it myself, with no
coding

10
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:30,572
background and no development team.

11
00:00:31,277 --> 00:00:34,651
As the name suggests, the whole thing is
organised around goals.

12
00:00:35,148 --> 00:00:38,667
I call them goals, but they’re really what
teachers know as learning objectives.

13
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:44,564
And the communicative goals are much like
what ACTFL calls can-do statements.

14
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:51,411
In the 1970s, the applied linguist David
Wilkins argued that a syllabus should cover

15
00:00:51,411 --> 00:00:55,997
the meanings and functions learners need to
express, alongside the grammar.

16
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:58,240
FluOLinGo uses both.

17
00:00:58,556 --> 00:01:01,134
Linguistic goals are about the language
itself.

18
00:01:01,559 --> 00:01:05,865
Communicative goals are about what you do
with it, like saying what you like or

19
00:01:05,865 --> 00:01:07,140
inviting a friend out.

20
00:01:07,493 --> 00:01:13,480
Later frameworks, like the CEFR, went
further and distinguished between sounds,

21
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,769
vocabulary, grammar and writing.

22
00:01:16,031 --> 00:01:23,104
In this talk, I split grammar into two: word
forms, like verb endings, and sentence

23
00:01:23,104 --> 00:01:24,940
structure, like word order.

24
00:01:25,203 --> 00:01:30,991
So I end up with five types of linguistic
goal: sounds, vocabulary, word forms,

25
00:01:30,991 --> 00:01:32,918
sentence structure and script.

26
00:01:33,687 --> 00:01:36,861
Wilkins was working on a framework for
European languages.

27
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:41,565
But could the same principles work for a
language as different as Chinese?

28
00:01:42,315 --> 00:01:44,531
That’s the question I want to look at today.

29
00:01:44,776 --> 00:01:49,723
Can a platform built for French work for
Chinese, a language that’s so different?

30
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:53,920
And if so, what can stay the same, and what
has to change?

31
00:01:54,237 --> 00:01:58,985
I’ll start with the French course, then look
at what makes Chinese different.

32
00:01:59,266 --> 00:02:05,036
I’ll show you how the same approach can be
adapted, using examples from both

33
00:02:05,036 --> 00:02:10,092
textbooks, and finish with some steps you
can try with your own language.

34
00:02:10,725 --> 00:02:12,978
This is where FluOLinGo began.

35
00:02:13,746 --> 00:02:18,133
It teaches beginner French using our course
book, L’Atelier.

36
00:02:18,432 --> 00:02:24,474
In the first version, I cut the course into
fifty small goals across five units and

37
00:02:24,474 --> 00:02:25,894
laid them out on a map.

38
00:02:26,554 --> 00:02:31,755
That’s the left side of the slide: fifty
numbered goals in a row.

39
00:02:32,524 --> 00:02:35,137
Every exercise belonged to a goal.

40
00:02:35,418 --> 00:02:40,166
In other words, I started with the goal,
then built the exercises around it.

41
00:02:40,374 --> 00:02:42,012
But look at three of those goals.

42
00:02:42,328 --> 00:02:45,376
Number twenty-eight, negation, is
linguistic.

43
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:51,120
Number twenty-nine, inviting someone and
turning down an invitation, is

44
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,124
communicative.

45
00:02:52,929 --> 00:02:57,008
Number thirty, writing an email, is a whole
task.

46
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:01,983
On the map, though, all three looked exactly
the same: one stop each.

47
00:03:02,607 --> 00:03:05,908
And that was a problem, because they are not
the same kind of thing.

48
00:03:06,351 --> 00:03:08,549
Negation is one small building block.

49
00:03:08,721 --> 00:03:12,981
Writing an email is a whole piece of work
that needs many of those blocks.

50
00:03:13,497 --> 00:03:18,254
So a learner moving along the map could not
tell a small step from a big one, or see

51
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:20,127
which goals the email depended on.

52
00:03:20,298 --> 00:03:25,924
At first, I didn’t notice this, because I
was simply following the French book,

53
00:03:25,924 --> 00:03:29,877
which already sorts its content into
situations and tasks.

54
00:03:30,103 --> 00:03:34,092
But when I started planning the Chinese
version, that support was gone.

55
00:03:34,716 --> 00:03:38,072
The Chinese book has no situations and no
tasks at all.

56
00:03:38,569 --> 00:03:43,209
That meant I had to decide, for every step,
what kind of step it was.

57
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:46,755
And that is when I saw that the map was
mixing three different kinds.

58
00:03:47,144 --> 00:03:48,998
On the right is the revised structure.

59
00:03:49,224 --> 00:03:54,886
Each unit now has two kinds of goal,
linguistic and communicative, and one task
at

60
00:03:54,886 --> 00:03:57,229
the end, where learners use both together.

61
00:03:57,618 --> 00:04:00,159
And you’ll see that same structure in both
languages.

62
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:04,211
Each linguistic goal gets its own lesson
page.

63
00:04:04,890 --> 00:04:10,461
Communicative goals, on the other hand, get
something different: four activities,

64
00:04:10,461 --> 00:04:14,613
one each for listening, reading, speaking
and writing.

65
00:04:14,911 --> 00:04:16,802
I’ll show you those shortly.

66
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:24,146
Sometimes, though, one stop brings in more
than one concept, in the same lesson.

67
00:04:24,427 --> 00:04:30,161
In Unit 0, for example, the days of the week
come together with the moments of the

68
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:33,480
day: morning and evening, for instance.

69
00:04:33,851 --> 00:04:38,754
That’s because beginners need both from the
very first lesson, and because they

70
00:04:38,754 --> 00:04:42,218
overlap: you say “Monday morning” in one
breath.

71
00:04:42,589 --> 00:04:44,135
Each page has six tabs.

72
00:04:44,452 --> 00:04:49,752
Learners start with the course path, then
move through explanations, forms and

73
00:04:49,752 --> 00:04:54,175
exercises, with bonus material and a word
list at the end.

74
00:04:54,763 --> 00:04:57,666
Let’s look more closely at one of those
tabs.

75
00:04:57,983 --> 00:05:01,899
Here is the exercise tab of another page,
for “to like”.

76
00:05:02,668 --> 00:05:06,223
It is a single sentence generator with three
levels.

77
00:05:06,829 --> 00:05:12,898
Each sentence starts from pictures: for
example, “we”, a broken heart and a piano.

78
00:05:13,106 --> 00:05:19,058
At one star, the verb is given, and you pick
its right form from a list of all its

79
00:05:19,058 --> 00:05:19,573
forms.

80
00:05:20,306 --> 00:05:25,090
Then, at two stars, you pick the verb and
the article together.

81
00:05:25,317 --> 00:05:29,378
Finally, at three stars, you type the whole
sentence yourself.

82
00:05:29,929 --> 00:05:34,560
And after that, the Bonus gives you the
English and asks for the French, with no

83
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:35,384
help at all.

84
00:05:35,628 --> 00:05:39,399
So what I learned here: one exercise, three
levels.

85
00:05:39,933 --> 00:05:42,674
Keep it on one page, with one generator.

86
00:05:43,225 --> 00:05:43,723
That’s it.

87
00:05:44,437 --> 00:05:51,239
Each unit combines linguistic goals and
communicative goals, which L’Atelier calls

88
00:05:51,239 --> 00:05:52,225
situations.

89
00:05:52,578 --> 00:05:57,806
As we’ve just seen, each linguistic goal
gets a lesson page, while each situation

90
00:05:57,806 --> 00:06:00,438
represents something learners can do with
the language.

91
00:06:01,152 --> 00:06:05,539
Sometimes, however, the book packs several
things into one situation.

92
00:06:05,783 --> 00:06:10,568
When it does, I split them up, so each
situation has one purpose.

93
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:13,227
But how do the two kinds fit together?

94
00:06:13,868 --> 00:06:14,946
They meet in a grid.

95
00:06:15,534 --> 00:06:19,848
Linguistic goals go down the side, and
situations go across the top.

96
00:06:20,056 --> 00:06:22,562
Here’s part of Unit 2, for example.

97
00:06:22,426 --> 00:06:27,391
“To like the” helps you say what you like,
and also say why.

98
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:32,836
Negation, for its part, turns up in several
situations at once.

99
00:06:33,135 --> 00:06:39,412
In other words, one linguistic goal can
serve lots of situations, and one situation

100
00:06:39,412 --> 00:06:41,411
can need lots of linguistic goals.

101
00:06:42,053 --> 00:06:47,552
So with the grid, I can see at a glance what
a learner needs before tackling each

102
00:06:47,552 --> 00:06:48,339
situation.

103
00:06:48,602 --> 00:06:54,372
This actually brings together two approaches
Wilkins talked about: building language

104
00:06:54,372 --> 00:07:00,233
up piece by piece, which is the synthetic
approach, and starting with what learners

105
00:07:00,233 --> 00:07:03,354
need to do, which is the analytic approach.

106
00:07:03,707 --> 00:07:09,640
Later, Janice Yalden called something
similar a proportional syllabus, bringing

107
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:12,109
structure and communication together.

108
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:19,001
Finally, every unit finishes with one task
that uses all its situations.

109
00:07:19,390 --> 00:07:21,425
L’Atelier calls it the Mission.

110
00:07:22,194 --> 00:07:27,241
All in all, in the new plan, the French
course has thirty-seven situations and

111
00:07:27,241 --> 00:07:29,059
around sixty linguistic goals.

112
00:07:29,819 --> 00:07:34,622
Let me take one situation from start to
finish, so you can see how it all fits.

113
00:07:35,174 --> 00:07:36,684
We’re in French Unit 2.

114
00:07:37,164 --> 00:07:39,235
The situation is saying what you like.

115
00:07:39,696 --> 00:07:41,315
You can see the French title here.

116
00:07:41,596 --> 00:07:47,475
First, learners work through the linguistic
goals using the three-star progression

117
00:07:47,475 --> 00:07:48,895
and Bonus you’ve just seen.

118
00:07:49,446 --> 00:07:52,838
On the left is the exercise tab from my
original page.

119
00:07:53,173 --> 00:07:57,985
Once they have the language they need,
learners practise the situation in four

120
00:07:57,985 --> 00:07:58,826
different ways.

121
00:07:59,269 --> 00:08:01,666
In ÉcouTexte, they listen to it.

122
00:08:02,055 --> 00:08:08,802
Next, in G-Compris, which means “I
understood,” they read a short everyday
document,

123
00:08:08,802 --> 00:08:13,886
like the message on the right, and answer
questions that check what the course has

124
00:08:13,886 --> 00:08:14,799
actually taught.

125
00:08:15,134 --> 00:08:18,598
In other words, spotting a matching word
won’t get you through.

126
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:22,216
After that, in WorDrill, they say it out
loud.

127
00:08:22,641 --> 00:08:26,449
And in ComposeIt, they write a few lines of
their own and get feedback.

128
00:08:26,603 --> 00:08:32,645
Finally, at the end of the unit, there’s the
Mission: planning a weekend outing

129
00:08:32,645 --> 00:08:33,215
together.

130
00:08:34,056 --> 00:08:38,732
Unlike the four activities, it uses every
situation in the unit.

131
00:08:39,067 --> 00:08:42,585
All along, on the map, learners see each
step in order.

132
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:45,751
That way, everything follows the same
learning path.

133
00:08:46,357 --> 00:08:49,730
Chinese has no articles and no verb
conjugations.

134
00:08:50,264 --> 00:08:55,628
Instead, what it does have is measure words,
aspect markers and tones.

135
00:08:56,288 --> 00:09:00,421
But you can still use the same five
categories for both languages.

136
00:09:00,755 --> 00:09:04,238
In fact, I’d argue they work for any
language.

137
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:07,241
What changes is how important each one is.

138
00:09:07,757 --> 00:09:09,937
Let’s start with what the two languages
share.

139
00:09:10,507 --> 00:09:14,550
French and Mandarin overlap on vocabulary
and sentence structure.

140
00:09:14,794 --> 00:09:17,173
Both use subject, verb, object.

141
00:09:17,417 --> 00:09:22,428
For example, look at these two sentences: “I
go skiing” and “I drink coffee.”

142
00:09:22,428 --> 00:09:24,029
They’re built almost the same way.

143
00:09:24,309 --> 00:09:27,520
Beyond that, though, they divide the work
differently.

144
00:09:28,198 --> 00:09:31,590
In French, word endings do a lot of the
work.

145
00:09:31,943 --> 00:09:37,533
In Chinese, by contrast, sounds and
vocabulary matter much more, and word order
does

146
00:09:37,533 --> 00:09:38,989
a lot of the grammatical work.

147
00:09:39,703 --> 00:09:42,480
So let’s go through the five categories, one
by one.

148
00:09:42,616 --> 00:09:45,935
First, sounds, which carry a huge amount of
weight.

149
00:09:46,505 --> 00:09:51,444
Tones matter from the very first day,
because if you get the tone wrong, you’ve
said

150
00:09:51,444 --> 00:09:52,212
a different word.

151
00:09:52,475 --> 00:09:55,957
Look at this example: one syllable in four
tones.

152
00:09:56,671 --> 00:10:00,877
Mother, hemp, horse, scold.

153
00:10:01,592 --> 00:10:02,921
Four separate words.

154
00:10:03,274 --> 00:10:09,045
So while in French a pronunciation mistake
usually gets forgiven, in Chinese a tone

155
00:10:09,045 --> 00:10:10,338
mistake often won’t.

156
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:14,607
Second, vocabulary, which is another big
challenge.

157
00:10:14,870 --> 00:10:18,605
For one thing, there are almost no cognates
to help you.

158
00:10:19,085 --> 00:10:25,362
And because of the characters, every word
has to be learned twice: once by ear and

159
00:10:25,362 --> 00:10:26,474
once in writing.

160
00:10:26,719 --> 00:10:30,219
Third, word forms, on the other hand, are
much simpler.

161
00:10:30,771 --> 00:10:36,234
For example, aspect particles do some of the
work that tense endings do in French,

162
00:10:36,234 --> 00:10:37,979
without the conjugation tables.

163
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:43,008
Fourth, sentence structure, by contrast,
matters enormously.

164
00:10:43,595 --> 00:10:48,752
Because Chinese has little inflection,
grammar relies on word order, topic and

165
00:10:48,752 --> 00:10:50,552
comment, and particles.

166
00:10:50,868 --> 00:10:52,397
Look at the pair of sentences here.

167
00:10:52,641 --> 00:10:53,880
It’s a classic example.

168
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:55,689
And here’s something interesting.

169
00:10:56,205 --> 00:10:59,796
Measure words give Chinese its own kind of
agreement.

170
00:11:00,130 --> 00:11:05,928
In French, every noun has a gender, and the
article and adjective have to match it.

171
00:11:06,805 --> 00:11:11,337
In Chinese, in the same way, every noun has
its own classifier.

172
00:11:11,617 --> 00:11:16,583
Here you’ve got a photo, a bottle and a
room, and each takes a different one.

173
00:11:17,207 --> 00:11:20,816
So you basically have to learn the
classifier along with the noun.

174
00:11:21,585 --> 00:11:24,126
In short, Chinese has plenty of grammar.

175
00:11:24,443 --> 00:11:29,137
It’s just moved out of the word endings and
into the vocabulary and the sentence.

176
00:11:29,671 --> 00:11:33,388
Fifth and last, writing is a different story
altogether.

177
00:11:33,886 --> 00:11:36,699
For Chinese, it’s a whole track of its own.

178
00:11:36,889 --> 00:11:42,442
But some languages, including many
Indigenous languages, are learned mostly by

179
00:11:42,442 --> 00:11:46,268
listening, so they have few script goals, or
none.

180
00:11:46,946 --> 00:11:50,953
So how do I take what I’ve built for French
and adapt it to Chinese?

181
00:11:51,559 --> 00:11:52,798
I do it in three steps.

182
00:11:53,477 --> 00:11:55,403
Step one: keep the frame.

183
00:11:55,756 --> 00:12:00,487
In other words, build the parts that work
for any language just once.

184
00:12:00,785 --> 00:12:05,660
There are also two important rules built
into it: the catalogue and the spiral.

185
00:12:05,923 --> 00:12:07,270
I’ll come to those in a moment.

186
00:12:07,695 --> 00:12:09,694
Step two: change the settings.

187
00:12:10,011 --> 00:12:14,135
That means the exercises still work the same
way, but learners make different

188
00:12:14,135 --> 00:12:14,759
choices.

189
00:12:15,546 --> 00:12:17,636
Step three: fill the slots.

190
00:12:17,844 --> 00:12:22,411
That means using the new textbook to work
out what learners need to be able to do.

191
00:12:22,692 --> 00:12:25,812
And this third step is where the teacher
does the real work.

192
00:12:26,129 --> 00:12:28,182
To picture it, think of a loom.

193
00:12:28,499 --> 00:12:29,901
The loom stays the same.

194
00:12:30,199 --> 00:12:31,076
That’s the frame.

195
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:34,568
But the thread and pattern change with each
language.

196
00:12:34,938 --> 00:12:37,372
Now let’s take the steps one at a time.

197
00:12:38,068 --> 00:12:39,217
Let’s start with step one.

198
00:12:39,606 --> 00:12:44,372
The frame is simply everything I can reuse,
whatever language I’m teaching.

199
00:12:44,598 --> 00:12:50,948
That includes the lesson pages, four types
of exercise for sentences, words, set

200
00:12:50,948 --> 00:12:57,116
phrases and sounds, the different difficulty
levels, bonus material, situation

201
00:12:57,116 --> 00:12:59,966
activities, unit tasks and the learning map.

202
00:13:00,698 --> 00:13:06,279
So for Chinese, the activities keep their
structure, but the texts and words change.

203
00:13:06,596 --> 00:13:11,172
On top of that, there’s one more thing, and
I actually only finalised it this week:

204
00:13:11,172 --> 00:13:11,923
the catalogue.

205
00:13:12,583 --> 00:13:16,644
The rule is simple: never teach or test
anything the book doesn’t cover.

206
00:13:16,870 --> 00:13:20,687
To make sure of that, I’ve designed the site
to check that catalogue in four

207
00:13:20,687 --> 00:13:21,529
different places.

208
00:13:22,315 --> 00:13:26,205
First, there’s a check that stops the site
from building anything beyond the course

209
00:13:26,205 --> 00:13:26,702
limits.

210
00:13:27,019 --> 00:13:32,256
Then there’s the path tab on each lesson, a
page for each unit, and the search.

211
00:13:32,554 --> 00:13:37,782
For example, type the French word for
“midnight,” the one you see here, and the

212
00:13:37,782 --> 00:13:42,042
search tells you: Unit 2, Situation 2.

213
00:13:42,666 --> 00:13:47,921
But type “half past,” and it tells you:
that’s not in this course.

214
00:13:48,365 --> 00:13:53,918
So I only need to build the system once,
then put the French or Chinese content into

215
00:13:53,918 --> 00:13:54,199
it.

216
00:13:54,768 --> 00:13:58,522
For French, the content comes from
L’Atelier.

217
00:13:58,784 --> 00:14:03,262
For Chinese, it comes from New Practical
Chinese Reader, Book 1.

218
00:14:03,994 --> 00:14:06,988
But how do I decide what goes into the
catalogue?

219
00:14:07,594 --> 00:14:10,461
And how do I stop it from becoming too
restrictive?

220
00:14:10,687 --> 00:14:13,410
The next two slides answer those questions.

221
00:14:14,106 --> 00:14:15,816
How do you build a catalogue?

222
00:14:16,132 --> 00:14:21,541
I use three sources: the textbook, the
teacher’s guide and the publisher’s unit

223
00:14:21,541 --> 00:14:22,003
tests.

224
00:14:22,699 --> 00:14:27,972
For example, if a unit test asks for
something, it counts as taught in that unit.

225
00:14:28,271 --> 00:14:32,793
So whenever I decide that something belongs
in a particular unit, I need evidence

226
00:14:32,793 --> 00:14:34,430
from one of those three sources.

227
00:14:35,072 --> 00:14:38,555
In other words, I can’t just include
something because I happen to know it.

228
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:40,309
Here are some examples.

229
00:14:40,174 --> 00:14:46,360
“Quarter past” and “half past” aren’t in the
French course, because the book tells

230
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:52,384
the time as “nine fifteen” and “nine
thirty.” On the other hand, “noon” and

231
00:14:52,384 --> 00:14:55,034
“midnight” are in, in Unit 2.

232
00:14:55,387 --> 00:15:01,230
Also, whenever the book introduces a verb,
learners practise it with all nine

233
00:15:01,230 --> 00:15:02,325
subject pronouns.

234
00:15:02,659 --> 00:15:08,502
And in Chinese, Lesson 11 teaches the time
with its own words for “a quarter,”

235
00:15:08,502 --> 00:15:13,938
“three quarters” and “half.” So the Chinese
course will teach them.

236
00:15:14,879 --> 00:15:19,139
Now, you might be thinking: so learners
never come across anything new?

237
00:15:19,564 --> 00:15:20,785
In fact, they do.

238
00:15:21,337 --> 00:15:24,584
That’s because the French approach I follow
is a spiral.

239
00:15:24,846 --> 00:15:30,707
In a spiral, learners meet things early, as
whole phrases, and figure out how they

240
00:15:30,707 --> 00:15:31,639
work later.

241
00:15:32,299 --> 00:15:36,143
So I’ve divided what learners encounter into
three categories.

242
00:15:36,406 --> 00:15:41,941
Seen means learners come across a phrase,
but they don’t have to study it or get

243
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:42,782
tested on it.

244
00:15:43,225 --> 00:15:49,005
Then, Focus means it’s time to learn it
properly, with explanations and practice.

245
00:15:49,322 --> 00:15:55,038
And finally, Scored means learners can now
be tested on it, but only once the course

246
00:15:55,038 --> 00:15:55,771
has taught it.

247
00:15:56,377 --> 00:15:58,448
To picture it, think of a camera.

248
00:15:58,891 --> 00:16:00,890
Something can be in the shot but blurry.

249
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:02,211
That’s Seen.

250
00:16:02,382 --> 00:16:04,092
Then you bring it into focus.

251
00:16:04,336 --> 00:16:05,177
That’s Focus.

252
00:16:05,476 --> 00:16:07,276
And only then do you take the picture.

253
00:16:07,610 --> 00:16:08,415
That’s Scored.

254
00:16:08,859 --> 00:16:14,665
In practice, learners meet “I was born in”
before studying the past tense, and “How

255
00:16:14,665 --> 00:16:17,243
are you?” before learning the verb “to go.”

256
00:16:17,505 --> 00:16:22,525
On top of that, I also allow a little
unfamiliar language, roughly twenty percent.

257
00:16:22,932 --> 00:16:25,637
That’s because people learn by struggling a
bit.

258
00:16:25,917 --> 00:16:28,531
If everything’s familiar, it’s too easy.

259
00:16:29,191 --> 00:16:31,733
If half of it is unknown, it’s too hard.

260
00:16:32,321 --> 00:16:36,762
But learners should never need that
unfamiliar language to get the answer right.

261
00:16:36,970 --> 00:16:43,193
To see the difference, look at the first
sentence: “At weekends, I go … skiing.” The

262
00:16:43,193 --> 00:16:47,779
answer is the word for “some,” so it doesn’t
matter that “weekend” is new.

263
00:16:48,041 --> 00:16:54,517
Now the second one says “It is two …” This
time, the answer would be “half past,”

264
00:16:54,517 --> 00:16:55,539
which is never taught.

265
00:16:55,910 --> 00:16:56,914
So that one’s out.

266
00:16:57,674 --> 00:16:59,962
Now for step two, the settings.

267
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:03,544
This is where French and Chinese start to
look quite different.

268
00:17:04,204 --> 00:17:09,269
Here’s the idea: I can keep the same
exercise, but change what learners have to

269
00:17:09,269 --> 00:17:09,749
choose.

270
00:17:10,101 --> 00:17:15,284
In French, for example, at two stars, the
key choice is the article.

271
00:17:15,890 --> 00:17:21,480
You like yoga, so it takes “the.” But you do
yoga, so it takes “some.” Look at the

272
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:22,556
two sentences here.

273
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:27,775
“The” versus “some” is exactly where
beginners go wrong, so that’s the gap the

274
00:17:27,775 --> 00:17:28,743
learner has to fill.

275
00:17:29,385 --> 00:17:32,071
Mandarin, however, has no articles.

276
00:17:32,496 --> 00:17:33,862
So what goes in the gap?

277
00:17:34,143 --> 00:17:37,607
In this example, it’s the verb that goes
with the object.

278
00:17:38,394 --> 00:17:40,356
These all come from the first textbook.

279
00:17:40,926 --> 00:17:46,145
Look at these three examples: play ball, see
a film, drink coffee.

280
00:17:46,516 --> 00:17:49,618
So the learner matches each verb to its
object.

281
00:17:50,387 --> 00:17:52,712
Another good example is the measure word.

282
00:17:53,426 --> 00:17:57,686
For instance, a piece of clothing takes the
classifier you see here.

283
00:17:57,949 --> 00:18:01,811
But the typical beginner mistake is to use
the general-purpose one.

284
00:18:02,399 --> 00:18:06,957
So I can reuse the same exercise format, but
change the answer depending on the

285
00:18:06,957 --> 00:18:07,545
language.

286
00:18:08,187 --> 00:18:12,249
In French, “some” fits and “the” bounces
off.

287
00:18:12,529 --> 00:18:17,666
In Chinese, in the same way, the classifier
for clothing fits, and the general one

288
00:18:17,666 --> 00:18:18,399
bounces off.

289
00:18:18,661 --> 00:18:23,654
Beyond that, Chinese also needs exercises
for tones and characters, but these still

290
00:18:23,654 --> 00:18:25,237
fit into the same categories.

291
00:18:25,825 --> 00:18:28,565
Now for the third step: filling the slots.

292
00:18:28,918 --> 00:18:31,550
To start, here are the two courses side by
side.

293
00:18:31,921 --> 00:18:37,212
French follows L’Atelier: a welcome unit,
then four themed units.

294
00:18:37,438 --> 00:18:39,437
You can see their French titles here.

295
00:18:40,188 --> 00:18:46,266
Chinese, meanwhile, follows New Practical
Chinese Reader, Book 1, with its fourteen

296
00:18:46,266 --> 00:18:48,663
lessons regrouped into five units.

297
00:18:48,944 --> 00:18:54,515
The French book names its situations, adds
short workshops built around real

298
00:18:54,515 --> 00:19:00,458
documents, which it calls expression
workshops, and ends each unit with a
Mission.

299
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:04,835
So for French, I mostly split up and sorted
what was already there.

300
00:19:05,279 --> 00:19:07,947
The Chinese book, however, has none of that.

301
00:19:08,354 --> 00:19:11,185
So I have to write the Chinese situations
myself.

302
00:19:11,501 --> 00:19:13,428
Twenty-six of them in total.

303
00:19:13,672 --> 00:19:19,768
As for the tasks, lessons 6 and 14 are
review lessons, so I use those as the basis

304
00:19:19,768 --> 00:19:20,754
for the final tasks.

305
00:19:21,451 --> 00:19:23,702
For the other units, I write the tasks too.

306
00:19:24,128 --> 00:19:25,476
Let me show you what that looks like.

307
00:19:26,037 --> 00:19:30,315
Take New Practical Chinese Reader, Book 1,
Lesson 3.

308
00:19:31,011 --> 00:19:36,293
The title is “What country is she from?” The
book gives you a text, some notes and

309
00:19:36,293 --> 00:19:37,605
pronunciation practice.

310
00:19:37,885 --> 00:19:43,113
But there are no situations, no task, and
this lesson doesn’t even have a grammar

311
00:19:43,113 --> 00:19:43,647
section.

312
00:19:44,072 --> 00:19:46,614
So I start from what the text actually does.

313
00:19:47,093 --> 00:19:48,224
Look at the first dialogue.

314
00:19:48,667 --> 00:19:51,208
First, someone asks who that is.

315
00:19:51,489 --> 00:19:53,813
The answer: that’s our teacher.

316
00:19:54,455 --> 00:19:56,852
Then: what country is she from?

317
00:19:57,350 --> 00:19:58,426
She’s Chinese.

318
00:19:59,014 --> 00:20:02,279
And then: all our teachers are Chinese.

319
00:20:02,668 --> 00:20:08,746
So from that short dialogue, I can identify
two things learners need to do: identify

320
00:20:08,746 --> 00:20:11,125
someone and say where they’re from.

321
00:20:11,514 --> 00:20:13,567
Next, the linguistic goals.

322
00:20:13,866 --> 00:20:18,090
First, the verb “to be,” and how to make it
negative.

323
00:20:18,334 --> 00:20:24,973
Then the question word “who.” Then the
nationality pattern: country plus “person.”

324
00:20:24,973 --> 00:20:30,490
And the word “all.” Learners have already
seen it in Lesson 2, but they won’t study

325
00:20:30,490 --> 00:20:34,208
where it goes in a sentence until Lesson 7.

326
00:20:34,759 --> 00:20:39,616
So for now, they just encounter it, without
practising it separately.

327
00:20:39,861 --> 00:20:44,627
Finally, one sound: the negative word
changes its tone before a fourth tone.

328
00:20:45,505 --> 00:20:49,711
So “is” and “is not” are said with different
tones.

329
00:20:50,353 --> 00:20:51,682
Notice the change here.

330
00:20:52,288 --> 00:20:55,481
As you can see, it’s the same grid as in
French.

331
00:20:55,798 --> 00:20:59,859
Only the content is new, and everything
comes from the book.

332
00:21:00,501 --> 00:21:06,471
In other words, I keep the same basic
structure, but adapt the goals, exercises
and

333
00:21:06,471 --> 00:21:07,836
situations to Chinese.

334
00:21:08,171 --> 00:21:11,798
And the textbook determines what learners
are expected to know.

335
00:21:12,513 --> 00:21:15,362
That’s how the French template becomes a
Chinese one.

336
00:21:16,022 --> 00:21:21,322
A word about how I built this, since the
title talks about an AI platform with no

337
00:21:21,322 --> 00:21:22,254
development team.

338
00:21:22,987 --> 00:21:25,709
I built FluOLinGo with AI coding tools.

339
00:21:26,008 --> 00:21:30,883
They’re fast, and they made it possible for
someone like me, who can’t code.

340
00:21:31,525 --> 00:21:33,072
But they can get the teaching wrong.

341
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:34,808
Here’s a real example.

342
00:21:35,487 --> 00:21:39,475
Remember the lesson page, with one exercise
and three levels?

343
00:21:39,828 --> 00:21:43,654
Earlier AI helpers broke that up into lots
of separate activities.

344
00:21:44,278 --> 00:21:48,909
Each looked fine by itself, but the teaching
idea behind it was gone, and only a

345
00:21:48,909 --> 00:21:50,040
teacher would notice.

346
00:21:50,393 --> 00:21:53,839
Since then, I’ve found two things that help
prevent that.

347
00:21:54,445 --> 00:21:59,618
First, I write down my teaching decisions in
plain language and make sure the AI

348
00:21:59,618 --> 00:22:01,111
always has access to them.

349
00:22:01,373 --> 00:22:06,709
Second, I look at the actual screen myself,
because the AI’s description of it can

350
00:22:06,709 --> 00:22:07,279
be wrong.

351
00:22:07,994 --> 00:22:13,584
As a result, those written rules help stop
the AI from making teaching decisions on

352
00:22:13,584 --> 00:22:14,207
its own.

353
00:22:14,416 --> 00:22:19,372
And because every rule comes from a specific
source, I can check whether the AI has

354
00:22:19,372 --> 00:22:19,924
followed it.

355
00:22:20,259 --> 00:22:22,927
In the same way, you can check my decisions.

356
00:22:23,280 --> 00:22:29,159
In short, it is absolutely necessary that
the teacher remains actively in the loop

357
00:22:29,159 --> 00:22:30,561
throughout this process.

358
00:22:31,302 --> 00:22:36,639
So here’s the recipe, for anyone teaching a
less commonly taught language or an

359
00:22:36,639 --> 00:22:37,733
Indigenous language.

360
00:22:37,869 --> 00:22:38,240
One.

361
00:22:38,882 --> 00:22:40,031
Start with your textbook.

362
00:22:40,637 --> 00:22:45,132
Identify what learners need to learn, and
what they need to be able to do.

363
00:22:45,412 --> 00:22:45,800
Two.

364
00:22:46,389 --> 00:22:49,419
Make a list of exactly what your textbook
teaches.

365
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:54,729
Keep teaching and assessment within those
limits, while allowing learners to

366
00:22:54,729 --> 00:22:57,216
encounter some new language along the way.

367
00:22:57,677 --> 00:22:58,066
Three.

368
00:22:58,383 --> 00:23:03,575
Give each linguistic goal its own lesson
page, and create a few exercise formats you

369
00:23:03,575 --> 00:23:04,307
can reuse.

370
00:23:04,660 --> 00:23:05,013
Four.

371
00:23:05,456 --> 00:23:08,631
Write your own situations where your book
doesn’t give you any.

372
00:23:09,020 --> 00:23:10,657
No tool can do that part for you.

373
00:23:11,263 --> 00:23:11,724
Five.

374
00:23:12,041 --> 00:23:14,076
One exercise, more levels.

375
00:23:14,682 --> 00:23:18,779
If something’s too easy or too hard, add a
level and keep the same screen.

376
00:23:19,512 --> 00:23:19,955
Six.

377
00:23:20,543 --> 00:23:23,428
Write down your decisions and check the
actual screens.

378
00:23:23,763 --> 00:23:28,927
Whether you’re working with AI or with a
person, the teacher remains the author.

379
00:23:29,624 --> 00:23:33,902
After all, French and Chinese are very
different languages.

380
00:23:34,200 --> 00:23:38,886
So if the same approach works for both, that
would be a good reason to try it with

381
00:23:38,886 --> 00:23:39,383
others.

382
00:23:39,935 --> 00:23:41,283
So where are things now?

383
00:23:41,671 --> 00:23:46,248
The French version is live, and planning the
Chinese version has given it a new plan

384
00:23:46,248 --> 00:23:46,601
too.

385
00:23:46,863 --> 00:23:52,562
As for the Chinese course, I am still
working out its design, including its units,

386
00:23:52,562 --> 00:23:55,175
goals, situations and exercises.

387
00:23:55,510 --> 00:23:59,526
If you are interested, you can let me know
and I will share about that when that is

388
00:23:59,526 --> 00:23:59,879
done.

389
00:24:00,250 --> 00:24:03,388
However, the build starts after this
conference.

390
00:24:03,813 --> 00:24:05,939
So I can’t show you a Chinese screen today.

391
00:24:06,274 --> 00:24:08,381
Let’s go back to my question, then.

392
00:24:08,897 --> 00:24:13,139
Can a goal-based platform built for French
be adapted for Chinese?

393
00:24:13,817 --> 00:24:16,322
From what I’ve designed so far, yes.

394
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:22,048
That is, I can keep the same basic structure
and adapt the content and exercises to

395
00:24:22,048 --> 00:24:22,744
Chinese.

396
00:24:23,043 --> 00:24:28,325
Of course, whether it works as well for
Chinese learners is the next question, and

397
00:24:28,325 --> 00:24:30,939
that’s what the actual build and testing
will tell us.

398
00:24:31,255 --> 00:24:36,266
In the meantime, if you teach a language
that you think can fit into this goal-based

399
00:24:36,266 --> 00:24:39,966
vision for a language learning platform, I’d
love to hear from you.

400
00:24:40,517 --> 00:24:42,552
My contact details are on the screen.

401
00:24:42,833 --> 00:24:43,764
Thank you for watching.
